---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import { SITE_TITLE } from '../consts';
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead title={`About · ${SITE_TITLE}`} description="Antoine Lépée builds the systems around AI agents: memory, harnesses, verification. And makes closed appliances observable." />
		<style>
			.elsewhere {
				font-family: var(--font-type);
				font-size: 0.85em;
				display: flex;
				flex-wrap: wrap;
				gap: 0.4em 1.4em;
				padding: 0;
				list-style: none;
			}
		</style>
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main>
			<h1>About</h1>

			<p>
				I'm Antoine Lépée. I build the systems around AI agents: the memory they keep, the harnesses
				they run in, and the checks that let people trust what they produce.
			</p>
			<p>
				I've been doing this kind of work for more than fifteen years, mostly by crossing borders. I
				started on the front-end, moved to back-end and business domains, touched data and
				infrastructure, and spent several years as an engineering manager. I joined Evaneos in 2016
				and I'm now an AI engineer there. I like the problems that sit between disciplines, and I
				like to carry them from the first sketch to the thing that runs in production.
			</p>
			<p>
				AI is not a recent turn for me. I started in 2022 with a conversational interface that
				helped travellers say what kind of trip they wanted. That thread led to Trip Designer, to an
				Evaneos app inside ChatGPT, and to what I spend most of my time on today.
			</p>

			<h2>What I'm working on</h2>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<strong>Tooling for coding agents.</strong> A pipeline of thin, independent layers that
					takes an agent from the intent of a change to the verification of its output, each layer
					useful on its own.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Personal OS.</strong> A memory for my agents that I own: plain Markdown, versioned,
					written during the session, caught up in the evening, consolidated once a week. The first
					posts here are about it.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>Making closed appliances observable.</strong> A
					<a href="https://github.com/alepee/hass-hitachi_yutaki">Home Assistant integration for
					Hitachi heat pumps</a>, used by other owners, and the instruments around it to understand
					what the machine actually does.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>An energy model of the house.</strong> A model that learns the physics of a home
					from its sensors, so heating, solar and storage can be planned instead of guessed.
				</li>
				<li>
					<strong>How Was Your Day.</strong> An app that records and transcribes meetings on the
					device, separates the voices, and writes a summary where every point links back to the
					moment it comes from.
				</li>
			</ul>

			<h2>How I like to work</h2>
			<p>
				Small increments that each deliver something. Tools that give teams more autonomy, not more
				process. Systems you can inspect: if I can't see what a machine or an agent remembers,
				decides or consumes, I don't trust it yet. I'm fond of the ideas of
				<a href="https://norabateson.wordpress.com/2015/11/03/symmathesy-a-word-in-progress/">symmathesy</a>
				and holacracy: learning happens between the parts of a system, and authority works best
				close to the work.
			</p>
			<p>
				The drawings on this site borrow the line of 19th century naturalists' plates. Those drawings
				had one job: show how a living thing is built and how it works.
			</p>

			<h2>Elsewhere</h2>
			<ul class="elsewhere">
				<li><a href="https://github.com/alepee">GitHub</a></li>
				<li><a href="https://github.com/sponsors/alepee">GitHub Sponsors</a></li>
				<li><a href="https://bsky.app/profile/lepee.me">Bluesky</a></li>
				<li><a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/alepee/">LinkedIn</a></li>
				<li><a href="/rss.xml">RSS</a></li>
			</ul>
		</main>
	</body>
</html>
